{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And Jacob said, Sell me first as of today, etc.</b> We need to understand his saying <b>as of today</b>, which has no [apparent] meaning. It appears that since this birthright that he is acquiring from him has particulars that do not exist in actuality except upon his father's death — and they are things that have not yet come into the world — and according to our holy Torah a person cannot acquire something that has not come into the world; therefore he was clever to say ״as of today״ in order that the sale should be valid. This is based on what they said in tractate Meitz'a folio 16a, and what the codifiers ruled (Choshen Mishpat, section 211, paragraph 2): a poor trapper who has nothing to eat, who said, ״Whatever my trap catches today is sold to you״ — his sale is valid, and the reason is because of כדי חייו (sustenance for his life). And the Geonim wrote that the reason does not depend on saying ״today״; rather, the same law applies for any other day — his sale is valid, for the reason of כדי חייו, and they only said ״today״ to exclude what his trap might catch thirty days or a year later, which is more than the sustenance of a single day. But [it is valid for] the sustenance of one day, in whatever form it may take. And this is the meaning of his saying <b>sell me as of today</b> — meaning, like the law of ״day,״ for he was exhausted, and because of כדי חייו his sale is valid even though it is something that has not come into the world. And without this consideration there is no legal grounds for him to acquire the birthright through purchase. And Esau justified Jacob's words, as he said, ״Behold, I am going to die״ — and there is no greater claim of כדי חייו than this."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:25.351791464Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}